<!--
  B.3 EID-11 pack: children of stock rule 61613 (Sysmon event 11, a file
  created) and of its one level-0 child, hand-ported from the Sigma YAML in
  phase-b-detection/sigma/. Phase B ISA F4. IDs 100405 to 100407 here; the
  other B.3 rules sit in their own pack files.

  How the port reads (each construct is in phase-b-detection/CONSTRUCTS.md):
    - every matcher is pcre2 and case-insensitive, as a Sigma string match is
    - |contains is an unanchored pattern and |endswith ends in $
    - |contains and |endswith on one field (100406, 100407) are one ordered
      pattern: the folder, then anything, then the extension at the end. The
      two cannot overlap, because the folder text ends in a backslash and every
      extension starts with a dot, so the one pattern says what the pair says
    - the decoder hands a rule each backslash of a path doubled, so a separator
      inside a path is written \\+ (one backslash or more). Sigma wants exactly
      one, so a path with a doubled separator in the live event also matches
    - the group names nothing a stock if_group matches. Stock rules attach to
      group sysmon_event_11 by if_group (rules in the 92200 to 92227 file, and
      91001 in the Exchange ProxyLogon file), and a pack rule in that group
      would take them as children and lose the final match to them
    - every rule names two parents, 61613 and 92216. Wazuh tries a parent's
      level-0 children first, and stock 92216 (powershell.exe writing a .tmp
      file under Windows\Temp or Users) is level 0: it matches on the text
      .tmp anywhere in the path, so powershell.exe writing a file called
      a.tmp.lnk ends at 92216 with no alert unless the pack rule hangs from it
      as well. 92216 cannot match a path under AppPatch\Custom (100405), so
      there the second parent is the idiom only
    - under one parent Wazuh tries the higher level first, and at the same
      level the rule loaded first, and the first match is final. Stock rules
      sit beside the pack rules under 61613, so a stock rule at level 12 or
      more is final over a pack rule on an event both match. Four can be:
      92211 (level 14, rundll32.exe writing a launchable file), 92212 (level
      14, powershell.exe writing a name with .7z, .zip or .rar in it), 92215
      (level 12, mshta.exe writing a launchable file) and 91001 (level 12,
      UMWorkerProcess.exe writing nearly anything). 92214 (level 15, an Office
      program writing a .lnk under AppData) is a fifth only for a name with
      .lnk inside it, such as x.lnk.vbs. Each such event still alerts, under
      the stock rule, and three cases pin 92211, 92212 and 92215. The other
      stock rules that match these paths are level 6 to 9 (92200 and 92203
      and their children 92201 and 92204 for a path under Users, 92225 for
      the all-users Startup folder), below every pack rule here, so the pack
      rule is final over them. Stock 92226 (level 14, powershell.exe writing a
      launchable file into the all-users Startup folder) is not final over
      100406 or 100407, though its level is higher. It is a child of 92203 and
      92225,
      both level 6, and Wazuh tries the rules under one parent in level order,
      so the pack rule at 12 matches first and the event ends there without
      reaching 92226. Such a write alerted at 14 before this pack and alerts at
      12 under the pack rule now. A test case pins it for each rule

  WARN: 100405 maps to ATT&CK T1546.011 (Application Shimming), where the
  upstream Sigma rule is tagged T1547.009. The test that validates it (Atomic
  Red Team T1546.011 test 2) is filed under T1546.011, and stock rule 92058
  uses the same id. The YAML's tag is changed to match, and its header says so.

  WARN: what the port does not carry. Nothing upstream is dropped from these
  three rules. The one difference is the doubled-separator tolerance above.

  Lint: bun tools/sigma-lint.ts. Tests: bun tools/logtest.ts.
-->
<group name="local,eid11_pack,">

  <!--
    SigmaHQ ee63c85c: a file under AppPatch\Custom or AppPatch\CustomSDB, where
    a custom shim database is kept. Upstream tests no extension and no image,
    so neither does the port: any process writing anything there alerts. The
    colon in front of Windows is upstream's, so C:\Temp\Windows\apppatch\Custom
    does not match.
  -->
  <rule id="100405" level="10">
    <if_sid>61613, 92216</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i):\\+Windows\\+apppatch\\+Custom(?:SDB)?\\+</field>
    <description>EID 11 pack: a file was created in the AppPatch custom shim database folder.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1546.011</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    SigmaHQ 28208707: a script or other launchable file written into a Start
    Menu Startup folder, the user's or the all-users one. The seventeen
    extensions are upstream's list. A .lnk is not on it; 100407 covers a .lnk
    written by PowerShell.
  -->
  <rule id="100406" level="12">
    <if_sid>61613, 92216</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\+Windows\\+Start Menu\\+Programs\\+Startup\\+.*\.(?:bat|cmd|dll|hta|jar|jse?|msi|ps1|psd1|psm1|scr|url|vba|vbe|vbs|wsf)$</field>
    <description>EID 11 pack: a script or executable file was created in a Startup folder.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!--
    SigmaHQ 92fa78e7: powershell.exe or pwsh.exe writing a .lnk into a Startup
    folder. Upstream's folder text is lower case and the match ignores case.
  -->
  <rule id="100407" level="12">
    <if_sid>61613, 92216</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\+start menu\\+programs\\+startup\\+.*\.lnk$</field>
    <description>EID 11 pack: PowerShell created a shortcut in a Startup folder.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1547.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
